To see whether the verification needs another look, run `git log --oneline b2228e62ef..HEAD -- packages/plitejs/src/authored packages/plitejs/test/support/authored-laws.ts packages/plitejs/scripts/authored-decision-order.ts`. The commit that added this check, `test(plitejs): check every decision order of the authored review tree up to a bound`, is in that range; it is the check itself. For each other commit it lists, read the diff and confirm three things. One call of `resolveWithVerdicts` plus `settleAsAdmission` still matches the resolve call in `decideOn` in `authored.ts`, apart from what What it does not cover lists. `admissionOf` still drops the blockers a call decides and reads the rest before placement, as `decideOn` does. Recording writes no mark kind or place the script lacks. Move the sync commit in this file forward only after that check and a run of `pnpm check:authored-order`. When recording gains a mark kind or a place, add it to `slotsOf` and `build` and to the spec's generators in the same change.

## What it does not cover

- For the quote documents under the plain schema, the public `decide` path in `authored.ts`: revision checks and `stale`, cards, decision tokens, `decideOn`'s `stores: true` resolve, withdrawals of held members, the `taken` check, the second blocker check for insertions inside removed content, schema validation of the decided document and undo. `settleAsAdmission` stands in for the run-scoped `resolveOwn` in `authoredAdmission`.
- Readable landed writes. The generated format tags link no earlier write through `over`, so no document in any bound tried has one, and only the children half of the comparison can fail.
- A known failure whose results change. A baseline entry names the document and the order, not the results, so a known failing order that gets worse stays green.
- What deciding a coupled set produces. No order of single steps reaches a coupled set, so nothing compares its at-once result. A planted bug that lets an accepted join merge into a block that another change in the same call removes passed the gate bound and changed 24 of the 68 coupled-set results. Other authored tests caught it with 34 failures, among them the sampled law `accepting every change at once equals accepting admissible batches in turn` and the preview reads of a block a pending join passes. To see which coupled-set results a resolver change moves, run the same bound with `--together=<file>` before and after it and diff the two files.
- Coupled sets beyond the gate bound. Only the gate's are in the baseline; a deeper bound prints their count, which Findings records.
- Splits inside a quote with a cut link. The script tries cut links only between top-level paragraphs.
- The script's own code. It is in no typecheck program, so keep the code that reads resolver results in the shared helper, which the plitejs test typecheck covers.
- Named roots and orphaned roots, which `resolveAuthoredValue` adds around `resolveAuthoredChildren`, except a card's body root of one line, which the generated-id variants build and the gate builds only under `elements` at one block.
- Shapes no bound builds, where the split is decided as it was before the fix. In one, the first container the split meets has a prefix slot, the decision lifts its children, or completion would rebuild it with no line, with more than one line, with a line that holds an element or with a line whose properties differ from the split block's. In another, the split block carries a format tag, pending or decided in the same call. Removing everything in such a container and rejecting the split right after it can still give a different document one at a time than at once, or refuse the second step as `invalid` while deciding both together applies. Suggested task t_674a5226 tracks it.
- For settle on load, any step that rejects, decides a join, split, wrap, unwrap or format tag, calls the id generator or drops a root. That leaves out a line deleted with its boundary, whose accepted join settle keeps unlanded, which suggested task t_e3dcf88b owns. Next's load of a verdict that deletes a card throws `Composing overlapping lifecycle changes for root rN requires the source value`, so the generated family skips a step whose decision drops a root until the construction-values plan's Open work item on that throw lands. The comparison loads through a fresh editor, so it never drives `settleBlocks`, which a peer's collaboration commit runs, and it leaves out the formats and contexts a settle lands. It lets a load keep a spare the decision drops when the step started with it, because settle keeps a spare while the build that would replace its block waits. So a settle that never drops a root record or a container's spare still passes. It cannot see a stale spare that the decision keeps too, such as the spare decide keeps for a join on a block the same decision removes. That plan's Open work lists both.
- Bold or other leaf formats, text longer than one letter, more than two blocks, marks or changes at the gate bound, schemas other than the plain one and the five generated-id ones, the generated-id family at any bound past the gate's, corrections that derive values, and collaboration.
- Sizes beyond the bound. A pass covers only the documents the bound builds.
- For the give-back family, everything past one link, one dropped key with no fill or generated value, one split and one change that removes the left half. That leaves out a chain of cuts, two changes that remove the left half, a removal of the left half's paragraph, a write to another key of either half, to another node or to another root, a change that removes a node holding another change's write, a structural wait such as a second split, five operations after the split, a proposed Enter that cuts a block, collaboration and recordings with random tag ids. Three push panel rounds on the reverted fourth build found its bugs by reading the source: a chain of cuts within a block and across blocks, a removal of the left half's paragraph, writes to another key, node or root, a removal of a node holding another change's write, a structural wait, a key whose fill differs by place, and five operations after the split. The decision log of `docs/plans/2026-10-23-giveback-order.md` holds each case. With random ids the same verdicts on two recordings of one script could settle differently, which the family no longer sees.

